description:
Write a small text layout like DirectWrite's, from the width of each character, with the hit-testing an editor needs:

```cpp
struct Caret { float x, y; };
struct Box { float left, top, right, bottom; };

class TextLayout
{
public:
    TextLayout(std::string text, std::vector<float> widths, float maxWidth, float lineHeight);
    int lineCount() const;
    int lineStart(int line) const;
    Caret caretAt(int index) const;                        // 0 .. text.size()
    int hitTest(float x, float y) const;                   // the caret index nearest to the point
    std::vector<Box> selection(int start, int end) const;  // one box per line, start < end
};
```

**Line breaking**: `widths[i]` is the width of character `i`. Lines break only after a space: the line takes as many whole words (with the space after each) as fit within `maxWidth`, where the **trailing spaces do not count** towards the width. A word wider than `maxWidth` alone is broken at the last character that fits (at least one character per line). `\n` always ends a line (it belongs to the line it ends and has no width).

**Carets**: the caret before character `i` is at the sum of the widths before it on its line, at `y = line × lineHeight`. The index at the very start of a line belongs to that line (so the end of a wrapped line and the start of the next line is the same index, shown at the start of the next line); the end of the text is on the last line.

**hitTest**: clamp `y` to a line (`floor(y / lineHeight)`, limited to existing lines), then find the caret position on that line nearest to `x`: inside a character, the left half gives the index before it, the right half the index after it (a "trailing" hit). Points left of the line give its start; points right of it give its end (before a `\n`, or for a wrapped line, the index after its last character that is not a trailing space).

**selection**: for each line the range touches, a box from the caret x of the range's start on that line (or 0) to the caret x of the range's end on that line (or the line's full width, trailing spaces included), from `line × lineHeight` to `(line + 1) × lineHeight`.

